In 1900, Lord Kelvin announced that two dark clouds were looming on the horizon of Physics. The two dark clouds later turned out to be the theory of relativity and quantum mechanics, which revolutionised Physics in the 20th century. Similarly, I hereby declare that I am a dark cloud looming on post-structuralism and the standpoint epistemology. I am a dark cloud that will revolutionise the understanding of marginalised experience. 

Standpoint epistemology posits that marginalised groups have a unique epistemic advantage in understanding social reality, as their experiences of oppression provide them with insights that are inaccessible to those in dominant positions. This perspective has been influential in feminist theory and other critical social theories, emphasising the importance of lived experience in shaping knowledge @wylie2003standpoint. Similarly, @Foucault2003Society[p:] proposed resurrect the "subjugated knowledges" as a counter to the hegemony of grand theories. Especially those at the bottom of the social hierarchy, which have been dismissed by dominant, hierarchical systems as "unqualified," "non-conceptual," "naive," or situated at the very base of the cognitive order. Foucault proposed to dismantle the monolithic discourse of dominant power through the rediscovery of these "subjugated knowledges", which exist outside the established regimes of "science" and "truth".

However, as I have argued in @sec:childhood-philosophy, my fondness for reason and science, which are often associated with dominant epistemic frameworks, are precisely rooted in my own experiences of marginalisation and oppression. My childhood experiences have led me to value reason and science as tools for understanding and navigating the world. 

I was born in a small town in a third-world country. I am not European, not white, not male (well, that depends on how we define "male"), not cisgender, not heterosexual, and not a coloniser. My mother was a textile worker, and my father was a machine worker. I was constantly excluded and bullied in elementary and middle school because of my personality and gender expression. After growing up, as a transgender biologist who supports gender abolition, I have become an outsider to all political camps. I have no group, no tribe, no community, no recognised identity to shelter me. The only thing I have is the courage to use my reason. It tells me that principles should apply to all people equally and without exception. The bullies at school could pull down my pants and throw away my shoes, the discourse police in online communities can distort my words and ban my account, but no one can stop me from, as Kant said, having the courage to use my own _reason_ to point out how arbitrary and baseless their rules are. If a child bullied for their gender expression is not a marginalized individual, then who is? If a child from a working-class family from the Third World is not a marginalised individual, then who is?

Insisting on logical self-consistency as the standard for understanding the world has been my most profound "lived experience" since my childhood questioning of housework division and kinship terms. The "harms of rationalism and universalism" they criticise have never appeared in my life and are completely disconnected from my personal experience.

Maybe I am the "dark cloud" in their theory. If I sincerely accepted this theory, I should refuse to accept this theory, because my own marginalised experiences told me to do so. Therefore, it is not that I choose to reject this theory out of ideological opposition originating from the "scientistic grand narrative," but rather that this theory cannot handle my personal experience. It would immediately throw a fatal exception and crash.

One may argue that the status of being marginalised is not a sufficient condition for having a unique epistemic advantage. It must be combined with a critical consciousness that allows individuals to reflect on their experiences and the social structures that shape them @toole2024standpoint. However, my own experiences have led me to develop a critical consciousness. 
The criterion for validity of an individual's knowledge should be whether there exists connections between their perspective and their marginalised status, and whether they have engaged in reflection upon this connection. It should not be a matter of whether their views align with standpoint epistemology itself. If only those marginalised individuals who agree on the standpoint epistemology are granted "epistemic privilege," this theory becomes circular.

Reason is not a form of hegemony. It is a small and fragile flame in this dark and cold world. It's worth doing anything to protect it.
What I have done is precisely like the anonymous prisoner in the Mauthausen concentration camp, using the powerful, rational subject constructed in the Enlightenment to resist the irrational violence of the world. Universalism and rationalism are not a claim to power. It is the requirement that keeps me alive. It is a relentless pursuit of a pure justice, a justice that can even empower an ultimate outsider. The goal of Enlightenment is precisely to use the light of reason to enlighten all the darkness of pre-modern superstition, privilege, exceptionalism, dogmatism, tribalism, and obscurantism, to create a world where there is no pain and harm, a world where no child will be bullied for wearing a pair of "wrong" shoes. 

I am the dark cloud. I am the storm. I am the thunder. 

I shall destroy all obscurantism, dogmatism, and irrationality.

I shall dispel all darkness, letting the light of enlightenment to shine forth. 

\

*_Veritas nos liberabit._*

*_Veritas nos liberabit!_*